#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""apply-status -- the ledger of a machine's dotfiles applies, and a read of it.

WHAT IT IS. The hooks that run around `chezmoi apply` (the base's tests/apply-suites.py and the
private layer's hook script) append one line per event to a ledger on that machine. A session that
cannot watch the terminal reads the ledger instead: `apply-status` for this machine's view,
`apply-status wait` to block until an apply lands. This tool owns the line format; the hooks call
`apply-status append` and never write the file themselves.

    apply-status append --source DIR [--hook] [--head H] <instance> <event> [detail]
    apply-status [status] [--json]
    apply-status wait --since <time> [--instances base[,overlay]] [--timeout D] [--interval D]
    apply-status head DIR

LEDGER. ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/dotfiles/apply.log, newest last, trimmed to its
newest 1000 lines on each append (under an flock on apply.log.lock, so concurrent appends and the
trim cannot lose a line). One line per event, seven tab-separated columns:

    1 time      UTC, ISO 8601 (2026-11-15T10:09:00Z)
    2 host      the short hostname
    3 instance  base | overlay
    4 event     start | skipped | refused | deployed | post-start | post-pass | post-FAIL | dry-run |
                by-hand | targeted
    5 head      the source checkout's short HEAD, `-dirty` appended when its working tree has
                changes (what an apply deploys), or `unknown` when git cannot say
    6 source    the source checkout's directory (so `status` can compare its HEAD now)
    7 detail    free text; tabs and newlines are replaced by spaces

EVENTS. `start`: the pre-apply hook began. `skipped`: the pre-apply suites did not run (detail says
why) and the apply goes ahead. `refused`: the suites failed or could not run, so chezmoi aborted
the apply (detail names the suites and the report). `deployed`: chezmoi wrote the targets (the
post-apply hook ran). `post-start`: the after-apply suite run began (before its first suite, so a
run that is still going is told from one that died). `post-pass`, `post-FAIL`: that run's result
(detail names the failing suites and the log). The private layer's hooks write only `start` and
`deployed`.

HOOKS AND `--hook`. A hook passes --hook, and the tool then reads chezmoi's context from its own
environment: with CHEZMOI unset the hook script was run by hand, so the event is recorded as
`by-hand`; with CHEZMOI_ARGS holding --dry-run (or --dry-run=true, or a short-flag cluster with n,
as -n or -vn) the apply is a preview, recorded as `dry-run`; with targets named on it (`chezmoi apply
<file>`) only those files were applied, recorded as `targeted`. Each way the real event moves into
the detail ("deployed: ...") and `status` and `wait` ignore the line. A targeted apply can run inside
a full one: the private layer's settings merge applies one base file while the overlay applies. Without --hook the event is
recorded as given. --head H records H instead of reading the checkout's head (a hook that wants the
head from before a long run). `apply-status head DIR` prints the head string a line would carry.

STATUS. For each instance: the events since its latest `start`, the deployed head, whether the
source checkout's HEAD now differs from it ("source is ahead of what is deployed"), and the latest
post result. A `start` with neither `deployed` nor `refused` after it is `incomplete`, with its age:
still running, or aborted for another reason. A `post-start` with no `post-pass` or `post-FAIL` after
it prints `post-apply running since <time> (<N> min)`; when a later `start` came first it prints
`post-apply did not finish (started <time>)` instead, since that run was overtaken or died. `--json`
prints one stable object; its `post_run` is null, or `{"state": "running" | "unfinished", "since",
"age_seconds", "minutes"}` for that run (`post` stays the latest result event). A ledger that never
had a `post-start` renders as it always did.

On macOS only, the view ends with an `agents` line from `launchagent-status --json` (15 s limit):
the LaunchAgent plists that are deployed but not loaded in launchd, or `not checked: <why>` when
that tool is missing, cannot tell (exit 3) or times out; `--json` carries an `agents` key, the
tool's own object or `{"checked": false, "reason": ...}`. It is a report only and changes no exit
code; on any other system nothing is added.

WAIT. Polls THIS machine's ledger every --interval (default 30s), prints each new event as it
lands and one `waiting` line per poll, so silence never looks like waiting correctly. It exits 0
once every instance named by --instances (default `base,overlay`; `base` for a machine with no
overlay) has a `deployed` after --since, 1 on a `refused` (an overlay never starts after a base
refusal), 3 on --timeout (default 30m). Each instance is judged from its LATEST `start` after
--since, so a refusal that a retry superseded does not fail the wait. --since is `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM[:SS]`
with Z or a +HH:MM offset (`date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` prints one), or epoch seconds; a time with
no zone is a usage error (exit 2), since local wall-clock time read as UTC lands hours off. The
same hand-written parser reads the ledger's own times on every Python. DURATION is a number with an
optional s, m or h (bare seconds). WAIT reads only the local file (STATUS also asks launchd on a
Mac) and builds in no ssh: a session reaches another machine by running
`ssh <host> apply-status wait --since T` as the WHOLE command (a sandbox exempts only a bare ssh,
nothing piped or redirected around it).

EXIT. append: 0 always, including when the ledger cannot be written (one line on stderr says so, so
a hook can never abort an apply); 2 a usage error. status: 0. wait: 0, 1, 3 as above; 2 usage.

WHAT IT CANNOT TELL. The ledger records what the hooks saw, not that the owner ran the full
command: an apply of one instance writes only that instance's lines, and an apply from a checkout
behind the forge shows as one whose head differs from the pushed one. A targeted apply is told apart
only through a hook (`--hook`), from chezmoi's own command line. A `start` is not a deploy. A hook that never ran leaves no line: a machine whose generated
chezmoi config predates the hooks (it gets them at the next `chezmoi init`) writes nothing. Another machine's ledger is not read here.

ENVIRONMENT. XDG_STATE_HOME moves the ledger. APPLY_STATUS_NOW (epoch seconds) fixes the clock
`status` ages a start against, for tests.

Standard library only, and Python 3.9 syntax: macOS's command-line tools ship that python3
(tests/test-python39-syntax.sh checks this file).
"""
import argparse
import calendar
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time

INSTANCES = ("base", "overlay")
EVENTS = ("start", "skipped", "refused", "deployed", "post-start", "post-pass", "post-FAIL", "dry-run",
          "by-hand", "targeted")
IGNORED = ("dry-run", "by-hand", "targeted")

# chezmoi's options that take their value as the next word (chezmoi 2.74, `chezmoi apply --help`,
# read 2026-11-17), so that value is not read as a target. --refresh-externals is not here: its
# value is optional and only ever joined with "=".
VALUE_LONG = frozenset((
    "--exclude", "--include", "--age-recipient", "--age-recipient-file", "--cache", "--color",
    "--config", "--config-format", "--destination", "--mode", "--output", "--override-data",
    "--override-data-file", "--persistent-state", "--progress", "--source", "--use-builtin-age",
    "--use-builtin-git", "--working-tree"))
VALUE_SHORT = frozenset("xicDoSW")
MAX_LINES = 1000
COLUMNS = 7
DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 30 * 60
DEFAULT_INTERVAL = 30


class Usage(Exception):
    pass


def ledger_path():
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".local", "state")
    return os.path.join(base, "dotfiles", "apply.log")


def now():
    fixed = os.environ.get("APPLY_STATUS_NOW")
    return float(fixed) if fixed else time.time()


def iso(epoch):
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


TIME_RE = re.compile(r"(\d{4})-(\d\d)-(\d\d)[T ](\d\d):(\d\d)(?::(\d\d)(\.\d+)?)?(Z|[+-]\d\d:\d\d)")
ZONE_HELP = ("give the time with a zone: Z or a +HH:MM offset (`date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` prints "
             "one), or epoch seconds")


def parse_time(text):
    """Epoch seconds from `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM[:SS[.f]]` plus Z or +HH:MM, or from epoch seconds; None
    for anything else. Parsed by hand, not by fromisoformat, whose accepted forms differ between
    Python 3.9 and 3.12 (one input must not be fine on one machine and an error on the other)."""
    text = text.strip()
    if re.fullmatch(r"\d+(\.\d+)?", text):
        return float(text)
    m = TIME_RE.fullmatch(text)
    if not m:
        return None
    y, mo, d, h, mi = (int(m.group(k)) for k in range(1, 6))
    sec = int(m.group(6) or 0)
    if not (1 <= mo <= 12 and 1 <= d <= 31 and h < 24 and mi < 60 and sec < 61):
        return None
    try:
        epoch = calendar.timegm((y, mo, d, h, mi, sec, 0, 0, 0))
    except (ValueError, OverflowError):
        return None
    zone = m.group(8)
    if zone != "Z":
        offset = (int(zone[1:3]) * 60 + int(zone[4:6])) * 60
        epoch -= offset if zone[0] == "+" else -offset
    return epoch + float(m.group(7) or 0)


def parse_duration(text):
    m = re.fullmatch(r"(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)([smh]?)", text.strip())
    if not m:
        raise Usage("not a duration (a number with an optional s, m or h): %r" % text)
    return float(m.group(1)) * {"": 1, "s": 1, "m": 60, "h": 3600}[m.group(2)]


def git_head(source):
    env = dict(os.environ, GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS="0")

    def git(*args):
        out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", source] + list(args), env=env, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, universal_newlines=True,
                             timeout=30)
        return out.stdout.strip() if out.returncode == 0 else None

    try:
        head = git("rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD")
        dirty = git("status", "--porcelain")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return "unknown"
    if not head or dirty is None:
        return "unknown"
    return head + "-dirty" if dirty else head


def flat(text):
    return re.sub(r"[\t\r\n]", " ", text)


def read_ledger():
    """The ledger's well-formed lines as dicts, oldest first; a missing file is an empty ledger."""
    try:
        with open(ledger_path(), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            raw = f.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return []
    rows = []
    for line in raw:
        cols = line.split("\t")
        if len(cols) < COLUMNS - 1:
            continue
        at = parse_time(cols[0])
        if at is None:
            continue
        if cols[3] in IGNORED:
            continue
        cols += [""] * (COLUMNS - len(cols))
        rows.append({"at": at, "time": cols[0], "host": cols[1], "instance": cols[2],
                     "event": cols[3], "head": cols[4], "source": cols[5], "detail": cols[6]})
    return rows


# --- append ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def cmd_append(argv):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="apply-status append", add_help=False)
    p.add_argument("--source", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--hook", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--head")
    p.add_argument("instance", choices=INSTANCES)
    p.add_argument("event", choices=EVENTS)
    p.add_argument("detail", nargs="*")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)
    try:
        write_line(args)
    except Exception as e:  # never abort an apply for a ledger problem
        print("apply-status: the apply ledger %s could not be written (%s); this apply leaves no trace"
              % (ledger_path(), flat(str(e))), file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


def is_dry_run(chezmoi_args):
    """True when chezmoi's command line (CHEZMOI_ARGS, space-joined) asks for a dry run: --dry-run,
    --dry-run=true, or a short-flag cluster holding n (-n, -vn). Nothing after a bare -- counts."""
    for word in chezmoi_args.split():
        if word == "--":
            return False
        if word == "--dry-run" or word == "--dry-run=true":
            return True
        if word.startswith("-") and not word.startswith("--") and "n" in word:
            return True
    return False


def is_targeted(chezmoi_args):
    """True when chezmoi's command line (CHEZMOI_ARGS: the program, then its arguments, space-joined)
    is an `apply` naming targets, so that only those files were applied. A target is a word after
    the verb that is neither an option nor an option's value; everything after a bare -- is one.
    The first such word before it is the verb itself."""
    words = chezmoi_args.split()[1:]
    verb = None
    i = 0
    while i < len(words):
        w = words[i]
        i += 1
        if w == "--":
            return verb == "apply" and i < len(words)
        if w.startswith("--"):
            if "=" not in w and w in VALUE_LONG:
                i += 1
            continue
        if w.startswith("-") and len(w) > 1:
            for j, c in enumerate(w[1:]):
                if c in VALUE_SHORT:
                    if j == len(w) - 2:
                        i += 1
                    break
            continue
        if verb is None:
            verb = w
        else:
            return verb == "apply"
    return False


def hook_context(event, detail):
    """What a hook's event is worth. Under chezmoi (CHEZMOI is set) a full apply keeps its event; a
    dry run, an apply of named targets only, or a hook script run by hand (no CHEZMOI), is recorded
    as `dry-run`, `targeted` or `by-hand` with the event in the detail, and status and wait ignore
    all three. A targeted apply is not a deploy of the head, and one can run INSIDE a full apply:
    the private layer's settings merge applies one base file while the overlay applies, and its
    `start` read as a new apply overtaking the base's post-apply run (measured 2026-11-17)."""
    if not os.environ.get("CHEZMOI"):
        kind = "by-hand"
    elif is_dry_run(os.environ.get("CHEZMOI_ARGS", "")):
        kind = "dry-run"
    elif is_targeted(os.environ.get("CHEZMOI_ARGS", "")):
        kind = "targeted"
    else:
        return event, detail
    return kind, (event + ": " + detail) if detail else event


def write_line(args):
    path = ledger_path()
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    host = socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    event, detail = args.event, " ".join(args.detail)
    if args.hook:
        event, detail = hook_context(event, detail)
    line = "\t".join([iso(now()), host, args.instance, event, args.head or git_head(args.source),
                      flat(args.source), flat(detail)]) + "\n"
    lock = os.open(path + ".lock", os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT, 0o644)
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)  # one append-and-trim at a time: a trim must not eat a line
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND | os.O_CREAT, 0o644)
        try:
            os.write(fd, line.encode("utf-8"))
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            lines = f.read().splitlines(True)
        if len(lines) > MAX_LINES:
            tmp = "%s.%d" % (path, os.getpid())
            with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.writelines(lines[-MAX_LINES:])
            os.replace(tmp, path)
    finally:
        os.close(lock)


def cmd_head(argv):
    if len(argv) != 1:
        raise Usage("head takes one directory")
    print(git_head(argv[0]))
    return 0


# --- status ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def post_run_view(mine, at_now):
    """The latest post-apply run that has no result: None when there is none (no `post-start`, or a
    `post-pass`/`post-FAIL` after the latest one), else its state (`running`, or `unfinished` when a
    later `start` came first), when it began and how long ago."""
    starts = [i for i, r in enumerate(mine) if r["event"] == "post-start"]
    if not starts:
        return None
    after = mine[starts[-1] + 1:]
    if any(r["event"] in ("post-pass", "post-FAIL") for r in after):
        return None
    began = mine[starts[-1]]
    age = max(0, int(at_now - began["at"]))
    return {"state": "unfinished" if any(r["event"] == "start" for r in after) else "running",
            "since": began["time"], "age_seconds": age, "minutes": age // 60}


def instance_view(rows, inst, at_now):
    mine = [r for r in rows if r["instance"] == inst]
    starts = [i for i, r in enumerate(mine) if r["event"] == "start"]
    since = mine[starts[-1]:] if starts else []
    deployed = [r for r in mine if r["event"] == "deployed"]
    posts = [r for r in mine if r["event"] in ("post-pass", "post-FAIL")]
    ends = [r for r in since if r["event"] in ("deployed", "refused")]
    if not since:
        state = "deployed" if deployed else "none"
    elif any(r["event"] == "refused" for r in since):
        state = "refused"
    elif ends:
        state = "deployed"
    else:
        state = "incomplete"
    view = {"state": state, "events": [event_dict(r) for r in since],
            "age_seconds": int(at_now - since[0]["at"]) if state == "incomplete" else None,
            "deployed_head": deployed[-1]["head"] if deployed else None,
            "deployed_at": deployed[-1]["time"] if deployed else None,
            "source": mine[-1]["source"] if mine else None,
            "source_head": None, "source_ahead": None,
            "post": event_dict(posts[-1]) if posts else None,
            "post_run": post_run_view(mine, at_now)}
    if deployed and view["source"]:
        view["source_head"] = git_head(view["source"])
        if view["source_head"] != "unknown":
            view["source_ahead"] = view["source_head"] != view["deployed_head"]
    return view


def event_dict(r):
    return {"time": r["time"], "event": r["event"], "head": r["head"], "detail": r["detail"]}


AGENTS_TIMEOUT = 15


def agents_view():
    """None off macOS (nothing is reported there), else launchagent-status's parsed --json object, or
    {"checked": False, "reason": ...} when it is missing, cannot tell, times out or prints no JSON.
    Exit 1 (a problem found) still carries the object; exit 3 and every other failure do not."""
    def unchecked(reason):
        return {"checked": False, "reason": reason}

    try:
        uname = subprocess.run(["uname", "-s"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                               stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, universal_newlines=True, timeout=AGENTS_TIMEOUT)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if uname.stdout.strip() != "Darwin":
        return None
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["launchagent-status", "--json"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, universal_newlines=True,
                             timeout=AGENTS_TIMEOUT)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return unchecked("launchagent-status is not installed")
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return unchecked("launchagent-status timed out after %ds" % AGENTS_TIMEOUT)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return unchecked("launchagent-status could not run (%s)" % flat(str(e)))
    if out.returncode not in (0, 1):
        why = flat(out.stderr.strip()) or "exit %d" % out.returncode
        return unchecked(why)
    try:
        data = json.loads(out.stdout)
    except ValueError:
        return unchecked("launchagent-status printed no JSON")
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return unchecked("launchagent-status printed no JSON object")
    return data


def agents_line(agents):
    if agents.get("checked") is False:
        return "%-8s not checked: %s" % ("agents", agents.get("reason", "unknown"))
    bad = [a for a in agents.get("agents", []) if a.get("state") in ("NOT-LOADED", "unreadable", "loaded-elsewhere")]
    n = len(agents.get("agents", []))
    if not bad:
        return "%-8s %d LaunchAgents, none deployed without being loaded" % ("agents", n)
    return "%-8s %d of %d LaunchAgents with a problem: %s" % (
        "agents", len(bad), n, ", ".join("%s (%s)" % (a.get("label", "?"), a.get("state")) for a in bad))


def cmd_status(argv):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="apply-status status", add_help=False)
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)
    rows = read_ledger()
    at_now = now()
    data = {"host": socket.gethostname().split(".")[0], "ledger": ledger_path(), "now": iso(at_now),
            "instances": {i: instance_view(rows, i, at_now) for i in INSTANCES}}
    agents = agents_view()
    if agents is not None:
        data["agents"] = agents
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(data, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        return 0
    print("apply status on %s (%s)" % (data["host"], data["ledger"]))
    for inst in INSTANCES:
        print(human(inst, data["instances"][inst]))
    if agents is not None:
        print(agents_line(agents))
    return 0


def human(inst, v):
    if v["state"] == "none":
        return "%-8s no apply recorded" % inst
    if v["events"]:
        first = v["events"][0]
        out = ["%-8s apply at %s: %s" % (inst, first["time"], " > ".join(e["event"] for e in v["events"]))]
    else:
        out = ["%-8s deployed (its start was not recorded)" % inst]
    if v["state"] == "incomplete":
        out.append("         incomplete: started %ds ago with no deployed or refused after it (still"
                   " running, or aborted for another reason)" % v["age_seconds"])
    for e in v["events"]:
        if e["event"] in ("refused", "skipped", "post-FAIL") and e["detail"]:
            out.append("         %s: %s" % (e["event"], e["detail"]))
    if v["deployed_head"]:
        if v["source_ahead"]:
            tail = "source is ahead of what is deployed (source now %s)" % v["source_head"]
        elif v["source_ahead"] is False:
            tail = "source matches"
        else:
            tail = "source head unknown"
        out.append("         deployed head %s at %s; %s" % (v["deployed_head"], v["deployed_at"], tail))
    run = v["post_run"]
    if run and run["state"] == "running":
        out.append("         post-apply running since %s (%d min)" % (run["since"], run["minutes"]))
    elif run:
        out.append("         post-apply did not finish (started %s): a later apply began with no result"
                   % run["since"])
    if v["post"]:
        out.append("         latest post result: %s at %s%s" % (
            v["post"]["event"], v["post"]["time"], " (" + v["post"]["detail"] + ")" if v["post"]["detail"] else ""))
    return "\n".join(out)


# --- wait ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def parse_instances(text):
    names = [n.strip() for n in text.split(",") if n.strip()]
    if not names or any(n not in INSTANCES for n in names):
        raise Usage("--instances takes base, overlay or base,overlay: %r" % text)
    return names


def outcome(rows, inst):
    """'refused', 'deployed' or None for one instance, from its LATEST start in rows (a retry after
    a refusal supersedes it); with no start at all, from every row."""
    mine = [r for r in rows if r["instance"] == inst]
    starts = [i for i, r in enumerate(mine) if r["event"] == "start"]
    mine = mine[starts[-1]:] if starts else mine
    if any(r["event"] == "refused" for r in mine):
        return "refused"
    if any(r["event"] == "deployed" for r in mine):
        return "deployed"
    return None


def cmd_wait(argv):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="apply-status wait", add_help=False)
    p.add_argument("--since", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--timeout", default="30m")
    p.add_argument("--interval", default="30s")
    p.add_argument("--instances", default="base,overlay")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)
    since = parse_time(args.since)
    if since is None:
        raise Usage("--since %r is not a time: %s" % (args.since, ZONE_HELP))
    timeout, interval = parse_duration(args.timeout), parse_duration(args.interval)
    if interval <= 0:
        raise Usage("--interval must be above zero")
    wanted = parse_instances(args.instances)
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    seen = 0
    print("apply-status wait: watching %s for %s to deploy since %s (timeout %s, poll %s)"
          % (ledger_path(), " and ".join(wanted), iso(since), args.timeout, args.interval), flush=True)
    while True:
        rows = [r for r in read_ledger() if r["at"] >= since]
        for r in rows[seen:]:
            print("%s %s %s %s %s%s" % (r["time"], r["host"], r["instance"], r["event"], r["head"],
                                        "  " + r["detail"] if r["detail"] else ""), flush=True)
        seen = len(rows)
        got = {i: outcome(rows, i) for i in wanted}
        if "refused" in got.values():
            print("apply-status wait: an apply was REFUSED; nothing further will deploy", flush=True)
            return 1
        landed = {i: got[i] == "deployed" for i in wanted}
        if all(landed.values()):
            print("apply-status wait: %s deployed" % " and ".join(wanted), flush=True)
            return 0
        left = deadline - time.monotonic()
        if left <= 0:
            print("apply-status wait: timed out after %s; deployed so far: %s; still missing: %s" % (
                args.timeout, ", ".join(i for i in wanted if landed[i]) or "neither",
                ", ".join(i for i in wanted if not landed[i])), flush=True)
            return 3
        print("apply-status wait: waiting, %s; %s; %ds left" % (
            iso(time.time()), "; ".join("%s %s" % (i, "deployed" if landed[i] else "not yet") for i in wanted),
            left), flush=True)
        time.sleep(min(interval, left))


# --- main ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def main(argv):
    try:
        if argv and argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__)
            return 0
        if argv and argv[0] == "append":
            return cmd_append(argv[1:])
        if argv and argv[0] == "head":
            return cmd_head(argv[1:])
        if argv and argv[0] == "wait":
            return cmd_wait(argv[1:])
        if argv and argv[0] == "status":
            return cmd_status(argv[1:])
        if not argv or argv[0].startswith("-"):
            return cmd_status(argv)
        raise Usage("unknown subcommand %r (append, head, status, wait)" % argv[0])
    except Usage as e:
        print("apply-status: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    except SystemExit as e:  # argparse's usage errors
        return e.code if isinstance(e.code, int) else 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
